ck mixed two concerns — \"compute one item's modified premium\" and \"accumulate across the policy.\" Extracting the per-item calculation into a named function (`calculateItemPremiumWithCurseModifier`) makes each piece self-documenting: the reducer now clearly reads as \"sum the per-item premiums,\" and the extracted function's name states exactly what it computes.\n- **Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: No duplication found elsewhere; not the driver here.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: Slightly increases element count (one more function), but this is justified by Rule 2's priority over minimizing elements.\n\n## Refactoring Applied\n\nExtracted the per-item premium calculation (base + curse surcharge) out of the `reduce` callback into a standalone function `calculateItemPremiumWithCurseModifier(item)`. The policy-level function now purely aggregates.\n\n```ts\nconst calculateItemPremiumWithCurseModifier = (item: ItemInput): number => {\n  const itemBase = MAIN_ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0;\n  const curseSurcharge = item.cursed ? itemBase * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  return itemBase + curseSurcharge;\n};\n\nexport const calculatePolicyBasePremiumWithItemModifiers = (\n  items: ItemInput[],\n): number => {\n  return items.reduce(\n    (total, item) => total + calculateItemPremiumWithCurseModifier(item),\n    0,\n  );\n};\n```\n\n## Refactored Code Mass\n\n`calculatePolicyBasePremiumWithItemModifiers`:\n- Constants: 1 (`0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 2 (`items`, `total`, `item`) → 3 = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (`reduce`, `calculateItemPremiumWithCurseModifier`) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (`reduce`) = 5\n- Assignments: 0\nSubtotal: 13\n\n`calculateItemPremiumWithCurseModifier`:\n- Constants: 1 (`?? 0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (`item`, `itemBase`, `curseSurcharge`) = 3\n- Invocations: 1 (lookup access) = 2\n- Conditionals: 1 (ternary) = 4\n- Loops: 0\nSubtotal: 10\n\n**Total Mass: 23** (was 20, delta +3)\n\n## Decision Rationale\n\nMass increased slightly (20 → 23) because spl